Positive Definiteness of Matrix
An n × n real matrix M is said to be symmetric if its transpose equals itself.
A symmetric matrix M is said to be positive definite, denoted by M > 0, if x⊤M x > 0 for every nonzero x. It is positive semidefinite, denoted by M ≥ 0, if x⊤M x ≥ 0 for every nonzero x.
• Every eigenvalue of M is positive (zero or positive).
• All the leading principal minors of M are positive (all the principal minors of M are zero or positive).
An m × n matrix H, with m ≥ n, has rank n, if and only if the n × n matrix H⊤H has rank n or det(H⊤H) ̸= 0. An m × n matrix H, with m ≤ n, has rank m, if and only if the m × m matrix HH⊤ has rank m or det(HH⊤) ̸= 0.
